finalizers
==============

1. Important Concept: Who Removes the Finalizer?
A custom finalizer key (e.g., [local.example.com/file-check](https://local.example.com/file-check)) does not execute logic on its own.
Kubernetes itself only sees string labels. For a finalizer to check a local folder and release the Pod, you need an active Controller/Watcher script running in the background.

Controller reconcile loop watching for deletion and evaluating conditions. Source: iximiuz Labs
  [ User runs: kubectl delete pod file-protected-pod ]
                           │
                           ▼
  Kubernetes marks Pod with 'deletionTimestamp'
                           │
                           ▼
  [ Watcher Script detects Pod pending deletion ]
                           │
  ┌────────────────────────┴────────────────────────┐
  │ Check Host: Does '/tmp/my-protected-data' exist? │
  └────────────────────────┬────────────────────────┘
                           │
             ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐
             ▼                           ▼
          [ YES ]                     [ NO ]
    Keep Pod stuck in          Remove Finalizer via API
       Terminating                     │
                                       ▼
                             Pod is removed from etcd
2. Complete End-to-End Demo Script
Save this script as custom-folder-finalizer-demo.sh and run it in your terminal. It sets up the protected folder, deploys the Pod with a finalizer, demonstrates how deletion blocks while the folder exists, and automatically releases the Pod once the folder is removed.
Bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash

# Exit immediately if a command fails
set -e

PROTECTED_DIR="/tmp/my-protected-data"
POD_NAME="folder-protected-pod"
FINALIZER_KEY="local.example.com/file-check"

echo "=== 1. CREATING LOCAL PROTECTED FOLDER ON HOST ==="
mkdir -p "${PROTECTED_DIR}"
echo "Important local files" > "${PROTECTED_DIR}/data.txt"
echo "Created folder: ${PROTECTED_DIR}"

echo -e "\n=== 2. CREATING POD WITH CUSTOM FINALIZER ==="
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: ${POD_NAME}
  namespace: default
  finalizers:
    - ${FINALIZER_KEY}
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: busybox:1.36
    command: ["sh", "-c", "while true; do sleep 5; done"]
EOF

echo "Waiting for Pod to be Running..."
kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready "pod/${POD_NAME}" --timeout=30s

echo -e "\n=== 3. TRIGGERING POD DELETION IN BACKGROUND ==="
echo "Running 'kubectl delete pod ${POD_NAME}' in background..."
kubectl delete pod "${POD_NAME}" &
DELETE_PID=$!

sleep 3

echo -e "\n=== 4. SHOWING STUCK POD IN 'TERMINATING' STATE ==="
kubectl get pod "${POD_NAME}"
echo "deletionTimestamp is set to:"
kubectl get pod "${POD_NAME}" -o jsonpath='{.metadata.deletionTimestamp}'

echo -e "\n\n=== 5. RUNNING CONTROLLER SCRIPT ==="
echo "The watcher checks if '${PROTECTED_DIR}' exists..."

# Controller Loop
while true; do
  if [ -d "${PROTECTED_DIR}" ]; then
    echo "[Controller] Folder '${PROTECTED_DIR}' still exists! Pod CANNOT be deleted."
    echo "[Controller] To release the Pod, run: rmdir ${PROTECTED_DIR}"
    sleep 4
  else
    echo "[Controller] Folder '${PROTECTED_DIR}' is gone! Removing finalizer now..."
    kubectl patch pod "${POD_NAME}" \
      --type=merge \
      -p "{\"metadata\":{\"finalizers\":null}}"
    break
  fi
done

echo -e "\nWaiting for background delete command to finish..."
wait $DELETE_PID

echo -e "\n=== 6. VERIFYING POD DELETION ==="
kubectl get pod "${POD_NAME}" || echo "Pod successfully deleted from etcd!"
How to Test It Interactively
If you run the script, open a second terminal window while the script output says Folder '/tmp/my-protected-data' still exists!:
Check the Pod status in terminal 2:
Bash
kubectl get pod folder-protected-pod
Output shows STATUS: Terminating.
Delete the directory to release the lock:
Bash
rm -rf /tmp/my-protected-data
Watch terminal 1—the watcher script instantly detects the folder removal, patches finalizers: null, and Kubernetes deletes the Pod immediately.
